# Human public docs

When writing or editing README, CHANGELOG, release notes, or GitHub release bodies:

## Voice
- Write like a careful student or indie developer: clear, short, factual.
- Prefer plain sentences over marketing or buzzwords.
- Do not sound like a chatbot summarizing a conversation.

## Never include
- Chat residue: “as you asked”, “as discussed”, “implemented what you told”, “per your request”
- Process leftovers: Bugbot / agent review tables, “vX candidate”, “UI polish continues”, apologetic screenshot caveats
- AI filler: “Premium…”, “Inspired by X, Y, Z…”, “seamless”, “robust”, “comprehensive”, “leverages”
- Fake roadmap theater: Sprint N checklists, “Phase 1” labels, “Critical” section titles unless the project already uses that for real planning
- Claims about unreleased features still only on a local branch

## Do include
- What the app is, who built it, how to run it
- Accurate latest release link / version
- Changelog entries that name user-visible changes (and real fixes)
- Known limitations that affect users (not emulator gossip unless useful)
- Download links for APKs when publishing

## Shape
- README: short intro → features → setup → links. Cut duplicate architecture essays if a `docs/` file already covers them.
- CHANGELOG: every shipped version, Keep a Changelog style, no “bumped version to…” noise.
- Release notes: changes + download. Drop internal review transcripts.

When unsure, read SkyTask’s current README / CHANGELOG / `docs/release/RELEASE_NOTES.md` as the tone target.
